k takes place more specifically  within the subcommittee's and you can  see the way the group voted to break  down the activity of cannabis we have  subcommittees on horticulture and  agriculture quality and math cloudy  management systems laboratory practices  processing and handling security and  transportation personnel training  assessment and credentialing as well as  terminology the executive committee is  consist of the officers the elected  officers of the committee which also  come from the stakeholder community  so in the process of forming the  committee and continuing at their first  meeting that was held last June in  Toronto Canada the topical areas that  were discussed as a need where standards  could fill are described on this slide  and you can see it's quite a busy slide  here I certainly won't won't read all  these topics but as you can see it's a  very thorough list of activities that  have been identified by the stakeholders  that standards could have an impact in  creating legitimacy for this industry  and that's the one thing that I have  noticed as I have inter interacted with  the variety of stakeholders in this  industry is they're very serious  scientists and and businessmen and  regulators and they recognize that this  industry has a need for legitimacy and  they see that being reached through the  formation of standards and and the  marketplace use of standards and that  makes me want to mention that one of the  reasons ASTM has been successful for 120  years is the idea of getting the right  stakeholders involved in the committee  having the right people at the table  making decisions on technical content  will result on what we refer to as a  marketplace relevant standard it will  have utility in the marketplace and the  industry will be willing to use these  standards as a way to improve the  quality of products and services and  taking into consideration safety for the  consumer as well as safety Keeney  environment this is an important aspect  of ASTM and committee D 37 on cannabis  seems to be well positioned with  with a great collection of stakeholders  that will be shaping the content of  standards and we're continuing to grow  and we're hoping that the folks that are  listening to this particular  presentation may have an interest in  participating and help shaping the  content of these standards for this  industry so I just would like to show  some of the activities that are  currently being pursued by the various  subcommittees in the Subcommittee on  cultivation and agriculture is working  on a standard practice for their  cultivation of medical grade cannabis  they're also working on a way to  classify the various horticultural  stages of cannabis the next subcommittee  D 3702 on laboratory is focused on  laboratory practices that would be used  to make sure that things like corrective  action and preventative action are in  place in the laboratory as they deal  with cultivation processing and testing  and distribution of the materials so  quality management systems will be  addressed in the Subcommittee on  laboratory working with the laboratory  subcommittee d 3703 on quality  management systems is focused on some  specific topics like developing a  standard test method for rapid analysis  of e.coli in cannabis products and  various quality assurance and quality  control measures that need to be in play  in the development of analytical methods  for laboratories subcommittee d 3704 on  processing handling seems to have the  the largest amount of momentum at this  time addressing activities of how  cannabis products should be packaged and  how they should be labeled  so the consumer can see precisely what  they are acquiring when they purchase  cannabis their addressing activities  like the role that water activity plays  in cannabis and and the importance of  that with regard to mold formation and  and any other kind of toxins that can  get into the plant they're developing  standards for extraction equipment and  methods for solvent based extraction  they're also looking forward techniques  for the refinement of cannabis extracts  and coming up with percents of the  various constituents that are found in  cannabis and making sure that that  information is included on the labeling  and packaging of cannabis materials an  interesting activity that was identified  I found that interesting had to do with  developing a standard on the  functionality of pressurized metered  dose inhalers which many of you may be  familiar with if you dealt with asthma  or if your children are dealing with  asthma or other lung issues the idea is  to make sure that these devices are  developed in a safe way and that the  components do not bring in any kind of  toxins into the delivery of the product  the next subcommittee is on security and  transportation and that the regulatory  community seems to be interested in in  these particular topics and a standard  practice for putting a video store video  surveillance system is is entering into  bow process right now there's also  standard practice being developed for  access control systems and also  intrusion detect detection systems all  these are important for the industry as  they move forward and evolve into a more  legitimate industry we have subcommittee  D 30 706 on personnel training  assessment and credentialing and right  now they are focused on working on a  standard practice for a certificate  program for the cannabis industry and  this will be around people that work in  laboratories and the qualifications that  they'll need to have to be considered a  qualified participant in a cannabis  activity so that concludes my  presentation on ASTM committee d 37 on  cannabis I hope that I generated some  interest from the community and we'd  certainly be interested in hearing your  questions and hopefully we'll have good  responses to them but hopefully will  stimulate your interest and have you get  involved in ASTM and help shape this  content so my contact information is  here ASTM as mentioned is a  international organization we have  offices in Canada Belgium Peru and China  and it's interesting to note that this  cannabis industry is truly a global  industry we had a well workshop in  Berlin last October that was well  attended had multiple countries  represented and they are excited to help  participate and shape the content in  global standards that will improve the  quality and products  services for this industry I thank you  very much for your time and I look  forward to hearing from you thank you  Robert for informative presentation we  will now start the live Q&amp;A portion of  the webinar so if you have a question  you'd like to ask please do so now just  click on the ask a question drop-down  box located on the far left of your  presentation window type your question  into the box that appears on your screen  and click on the send button will answer  as many of your questions as we have  time for so let's get started our first  question is how our ASTM standards used  in the marketplace okay well as TM  standards as I as I mentioned are used  on a daily basis in the marketplace all  products that you can just about think  of that are in your home that your home  is made of the fuel for your car and  airplanes children's toys they all come  into play in making sure that the  quality of these products and services  are at the highest they can be while  also being safe for use so these  standards are developed by volunteer  members on the technical committees and  then they're used voluntarily by the  marketplace and the good news as I  mentioned if you have the right people  at the table making the decisions you'll  have a marketplace relevant document  that will have desire to be used also  they find their way into government  regulations as I stated and thousands of  ASTM standards are used to assist in  help shaping the content of regulations  again for keeping a safe environment or  protecting the consumer in one way or  another so ASTM standards are used daily  in the marketplace by by companies  around the world thank you for that  Robert how are ASTM standards different  from government regulations  well government regulate  stations are mandatory documents there  law and and they have to be followed  ASTM standards again are developed by  volunteers on our committees and when  they get into the marketplace they do  not necessarily have to be followed if  they're not part of some kind of mandate  in a contract or by a regulation it's up  to the marketplace to determine whether  they want to use the standard or not and  as I mentioned the good news is because  of the people and the commitment of the  volunteers and the stakeholders the end  result are standards that had utility  and the marketplace is driven to use  them because of the impact they have on  the products and services that are in  play with regard to the standards I  would like to once again thank Robert  Morgan for his presentation I would also  like to thank leverets for making  today's educational webcast possible  before we go I want to let everyone know  that today's webcast will be available  for on-demand viewing through June 29  2018 you'll receive an email from lab  roots letting you know when this webcast  will be available for replay please  share that announcement with your  colleagues who may have missed today's  live event that is all for now thank you  for joining us we hope to feel again  soon bye
 

